\itemize{
\item \code{"response"} (default): draws from the posterior predictive
distribution on the outcome scale. Routes through
\code{\link[=posterior_predict.mvgam]{posterior_predict.mvgam()}}.
\item \code{"link"}: linear predictor draws on the link scale (no
inverse-link applied). Routes through
\code{\link[=posterior_linpred.mvgam]{posterior_linpred.mvgam()}}.
\item \code{"expected"}: posterior expectation E[Y | X] on the response
scale. Routes through \code{\link[=posterior_epred.mvgam]{posterior_epred.mvgam()}}.
\item \code{"variance"}: conditional variance Var[Y | theta] per draw,
using family-specific mean-variance formulas. Supported
families: gaussian, student, lognormal, poisson, bernoulli,
binomial, negbinomial, gamma, beta. Other families raise an
informative error. Under the default the variance is
conditional on the parameter draw alone, so it is the
observation noise around the mean the two submodels give.
\code{process_error = TRUE} conditions on that draw's sampled
trend innovation as well, giving the observation noise at
the trend-integrated mean.
\item \code{"terms"}: accepted so that a call carried over from
\code{\link[mgcv:predict.gam]{mgcv::predict.gam()}} is answered rather than silently
mismatched. mvgam splits the decomposition across
\code{\link[=posterior_smooths.mvgam]{posterior_smooths.mvgam()}} for per-smooth draws and
\code{\link[=fixef.mvgam]{fixef.mvgam()}} / \code{\link[=ranef.mvgam]{ranef.mvgam()}} for the parametric and
random-effect components, so the call errors with a pointer
at those.
\item \code{"latent_state"}: closure-unit families. Family-aware
posterior of the latent state per closure unit, conditioned
on the observed detection history:
\itemize{
\item \code{nmix()}: posterior latent abundance \code{N}
conditional on the observed visit counts, via
\code{posterior_latent_N()} (Royle 2004).
\item \code{occ()}: posterior occupancy probability
\code{P(z = 1 | y_g)} via \code{posterior_occupancy()}. Returns
the probability matrix by default; use
\code{posterior_occupancy(draw = TRUE)} directly for 0/1 z
draws.
